#{[17592186761910 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761910 :tutorial/content "**MTH 256 — Recitation 7: Quiz 3 Review**\n\nDue: unknown\nDate imported: 2026-11-13\nSource: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review.pdf)\nAnswer key: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review Key.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review%20Key.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions. Neither PDF specifies a due date.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThe recitation this week serves as a review for quiz three, which will be taken in the latter half of recitation time. It also prepares you for the wording on the quiz. The quiz covers Sections 5.1 and 5.2 and Euler equations from Section 7.4. The following definition and theorems will be provided on the quiz (source PDF p. 1).\n\n### Wronskian definition\n\nFor two functions $\\{y_1(x),y_2(x)\\}$,\n$$W(x)=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2.$$\n\n**Topics:** [Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.1.%20Theory%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Linear%20ODEs/Lessons/3.1.6.%20Linear%20Independence%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Homogeneous%20ODEs.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 5.2.1 — Constant coefficients\n\nLet $p(r)=ar^2+br+c$ be the characteristic polynomial of\n$$ay''+by'+cy=0.\\tag{5.2.20}$$\n\n(a) If $p(r)=0$ has distinct real roots $r_1,r_2$, then the general solution is\n$$y=c_1e^{r_1x}+c_2e^{r_2x}.$$\n\n(b) If it has a repeated root $r_1$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{r_1x}(c_1+c_2x).$$\n\n(c) If it has complex conjugate roots $r_1=\\lambda+i\\omega$ and $r_2=\\lambda-i\\omega$, where $\\omega>0$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{\\lambda x}(c_1\\cos\\omega x+c_2\\sin\\omega x).$$\n\n**Topics:** [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.1.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.2.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.3.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 7.4.3 — Euler equations\n\nSuppose the roots of the indicial equation\n$$ar(r-1)+br+c=0\\tag{7.4.11}$$\nare $r_1,r_2$. Then the general solution of the Euler equation\n$$ax^2y''+bxy'+cy=0\\tag{7.4.12}$$\non $(0,\\infty)$ is\n\n- $y=c_1x^{r_1}+c_2x^{r_2}$ if $r_1,r_2$ are distinct real numbers;\n- $y=x^{r_1}(c_1+c_2\\ln x)$ if $r_1=r_2$;\n- $y=x^\\lambda[c_1\\cos(\\omega\\ln x)+c_2\\sin(\\omega\\ln x)]$ if $r_1,r_2=\\lambda\\pm i\\omega$ with $\\omega>0$.\n\n**Topics:** [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.1.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.2.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.3.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f97993b-9e3a-5621-807f-4cb2646d6ab5" :tutorial/title "Assignment information"}] [17592186761917 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df1ec775-25c8-5177-a130-e1547608717a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1x+C_2x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6be089-872c-5fc5-8863-8711b543e132" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761923}] :question/id #uuid "228f22c4-9d80-5d95-bd19-73b3f5bad258" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 5, Problem 10.\n\nThe Euler equation\n$$x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$$\nhas indicial equation $r^2+2r-3=0$, with roots $r_1=1$ and $r_2=-3$. Find the general solution for $0<x<\\infty$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "The distinct real roots $r_1=1$ and $r_2=-3$ give the independent solutions $x^{r_1}=x$ and $x^{r_2}=x^{-3}$. Therefore $y=C_1x+C_2x^{-3}$ on $(0,\\infty)$. To check, substituting $y=x^r$ gives $r(r-1)+3r-3=(r-1)(r+3)=0$, which both exponents satisfy."}] [17592186761927 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$,\n$$y_1'=-3x^{-4},\\qquad y_1''=12x^{-5},\\qquad y_2'=1,\\qquad y_2''=0.$$\nSubstitution into $L[y]=x^2y''+3xy'-3y$ gives\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-9x^{-3}-3x^{-3}=0,\\qquad L[x]=3x-3x=0.$$\nThus both functions solve the equation. Their Wronskian is\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThe normalized coefficients $3/x$ and $-3/x^2$ are continuous on $(0,\\infty)$. Since the two solutions are linearly independent there, they form a fundamental set of solutions on that interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0ff44b-2295-53ff-a2de-b5a8b8c914e1" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761946}] :question/id #uuid "3b51ca7a-7572-534e-b8b8-82d1a8af6ccb" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 2, Problem 1.\n\nThe functions $x^{-3}$ and $x$ solve\n$$x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0.$$\nShow that $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ form a fundamental set of solutions for $0<x<\\infty$. Your solution must include a Wronskian calculation." :question/worked-solution "Check both requirements: each function satisfies the ODE, and the Wronskian is nonzero on the stated interval. The minus sign in $y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2$ changes the negative derivative term into $+3x^{-3}$, giving $W=4x^{-3}$."}] [17592186761948 {:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$,\n$$y_1'=-3x^{-4},\\qquad y_1''=12x^{-5},\\qquad y_2'=1,\\qquad y_2''=0.$$\nSubstitution into $L[y]=x^2y''+3xy'-3y$ gives\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-9x^{-3}-3x^{-3}=0,\\qquad L[x]=3x-3x=0.$$\nThus both functions solve the equation. Their Wronskian is\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThe normalized coefficients $3/x$ and $-3/x^2$ are continuous on $(0,\\infty)$. Since the two solutions are linearly independent there, they form a fundamental set of solutions on that interval." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}]}
